<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  CyberpunkVRPort. Take photo mode off the pad, leave it on the keyboard.

  WHY. The game binds TogglePhotoModeButton to IK_N and to IK_PAD_LR_THUMB, and that second one is
  both stick clicks with no time window on it. This port synthesises LEFT_THUMB for sprint
  (XInput.cpp, wantSprint) and RIGHT_THUMB for the right stick down crouch gesture (wantCrouch), so
  sliding sends the game exactly that chord and photo mode opens on its own. The screen goes flat,
  camera controls appear, and the way out is not obvious in a headset.

  WHY THE BINDING AND NOT A BUTTON. Suppressing the two clicks from co-occurring was the first idea
  and it is wrong: sprint plus crouch IS the slide, so that trade buys a fix by removing a move the
  player wants. R3 cannot go either, because it carries the crouch gesture and also QuickMelee,
  Tag, Dive and PickUpBodyFromTakedown. The chord is the only part nobody asked for.

  N still opens photo mode, and ExitPhotoModeButton keeps IK_Escape, IK_N and IK_Pad_B_CIRCLE, so
  anyone already inside it can still get out with Circle.

  NOTE ON STYLE: no double hyphen anywhere in these comments. XML forbids it inside a comment and
  the game parser swallows that quietly, which is the worst way to be wrong.

  UNVERIFIED AS OF WRITING. input_loader merges r6\input\*.xml into r6\cache\inputUserMappings.xml
  at launch, and its log shows only merge operations, never a removal. Whether a mod mapping that
  reuses an existing name REPLACES the game entry or is appended to it is exactly what this file
  measures. After one launch, read TogglePhotoModeButton out of the merged cache: ONE button means
  replace, and this works. TWO means append, and it does not, in which case delete this file and
  the binding has to be dealt with in redscript instead.
-->
<bindings>
	<mapping name="TogglePhotoModeButton" type="Button">
		<button id="IK_N" overridableUI="photoMode" />
	</mapping>
</bindings>
